<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander of Battenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Alexander I of Bulgaria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander of Battenberg was a German prince elected the first ruler of the autonomous Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1879. He accepted the union with Eastern Rumelia in 1885 and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> at Slivnitsa that year, but his independence angered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; pro-Russian officers forced his abdication in 1886.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg.jpg" alt="Alexander of Battenberg" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dimitar Karastoyanov · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_I_of_Bulgaria_by_Dimitar_Karastoyanov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander was the second son of Prince Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and his morganatic wife, Countess Julia von Hauke. He was a nephew of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>, who was much attached to him, and in boyhood he often visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. He accompanied the tsar during the Bulgarian campaign of 1877. His brothers married into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>'s family, and their descendants included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Earl Mountbatten of Burma</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> became an autonomous principality under Ottoman suzerainty by the Treaty of Berlin in 1878, the tsar recommended his nephew for the throne, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-national-assembly-of-turkey">Grand National Assembly</a> elected him unanimously on 29 April 1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He had no experience of government and was caught between the Russians, who wanted a passive prince, and quarrelling Bulgarian politicians. With the tsar's consent he suspended the constitution in 1881, but real power passed to two Russian generals, and he restored it in 1883, which led to a serious breach with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The revolution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Plovdiv</a> on 18 September 1885 united Eastern Rumelia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> with his consent. Russia withdrew its officers from the Bulgarian army, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> invaded. Alexander rallied the army, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> won a decisive victory at Slivnitsa on 19 November and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a> on 27 November. Austria's intervention protected Serbia from the consequences, but the union with Eastern Rumelia was sealed.</p><p>Discontented officers, encouraged by Tsar Alexander III, seized the prince in the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> on the night of 20 August 1886 and forced him to sign his abdication. A counter-revolution led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-stambolov">Stefan Stambolov</a> allowed him to return, but his cousin the tsar refused his return, and he resigned the throne and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> on 8 September 1886.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alexander afterwards lived privately and in 1889 married the actress Johanna Loisinger, taking the style Count von Hartenau. He held a local command in the Austrian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, where he died of a ruptured appendix on 17 November 1893. His body was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, given a public funeral and buried in a mausoleum built in his memory. Battenberg Hill on Livingston Island in Antarctica is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander of Battenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The battle near Slivnitsa, west of Sofia, which began on 17 November, ended with the Bulgarian army under Prince <b>Alexander of Battenberg</b> defeating the invading Serbian army of King Milan I. The victory secured Bulgaria's union with Eastern Rumelia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-19">19 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1879 the Tarnovo Constitution was adopted and <b>Alexander of Battenberg</b> elected prince, with Sofia as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Verona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1880" aria-label="Verona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alexander of Battenberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 6177h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 6177h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,10.99&amp;year=1880">What was here in 1880? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
